</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<p>
The next two problems repeat the three questions of
<xref ref="ex-skel-two-runners"/> in two more physical settings: find the
direction of most rapid decrease, the rate along a prescribed direction,
and the directions of no change.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="ex-electrostatic-plate">
  <title>Electric Potential on a Coated Plate</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      The electric potential on a thin coated plate, in volts, is
      <md>V(x,y) = 100 - 2x^2 - 6y^2,</md>
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in centimeters. Consider the point <m>P_0(2,1)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find <m>\nabla V</m> at <m>P_0</m> and the electric field
            <m>\mathbf E = -\nabla V</m> there. Along which unit vector does the
            potential drop most rapidly, and at what rate?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A test charge is nudged along
            <m>\hat{u} = \tfrac{4}{5}\hat{\imath} - \tfrac{3}{5}\hat{\jmath}</m>. Compute
            <m>D_{\hat{u}}V</m> at <m>P_0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the two unit vectors along which the potential does not change, and name
            the curve they are tangent to.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
  </statement>
  <!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\nabla V = -4x\,\hat{\imath} - 12y\,\hat{\jmath}</m>, so
            <m>(\nabla V)_{(2,1)} = -8\,\hat{\imath} - 12\,\hat{\jmath}</m> and
            <m>\mathbf E = 8\,\hat{\imath} + 12\,\hat{\jmath}</m> V/cm. The potential drops
            fastest along <m>\hat{u} = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{13}}(2\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath})</m>,
            at the rate <m>-\|\nabla V\| = -4\sqrt{13} \approx -14.4</m> V/cm.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>D_{\hat{u}}V = (-8)\left(\tfrac{4}{5}\right)
                + (-12)\left(-\tfrac{3}{5}\right) = \tfrac{4}{5} = 0.8</md>
            in V/cm. The potential rises slightly along this heading.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\hat{u} = \pm\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{13}}(3\,\hat{\imath} - 2\,\hat{\jmath})</m>. These are
            tangent to the equipotential ellipse <m>2x^2 + 6y^2 = 14</m> through <m>P_0</m>,
            and they are orthogonal to <m>\mathbf E</m>, as they must be.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p component="print">
      An interactive figure of the plate <mdash/> the equipotential route at
      <m>V = 86</m> V and the field-line route side by side, with the
      potential read out as the point moves <mdash/> accompanies the online
      version of these notes.
    </p>
    <sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
    <figure xml:id="figure-potential-plate">
      <caption>The potential <m>V(x,y) = 100 - 2x^2 - 6y^2</m> on the plate. One route holds <m>V = 86</m> V along the equipotential <m>2x^2 + 6y^2 = 14</m>; the other follows a field line, on which <m>D_{\hat{u}}V = -\|\nabla V\|</m> at every point.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-potential-plate"
                   iframe="potential-plate-3d.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="16:10"/>
    </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </answer>
  END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-isobars-low">
  <title>Sailing the Isobars of a Low</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Near the center of a low-pressure system the sea-level pressure, in millibars, is
      modeled by
      <md>P(x,y) = 988 + 0.004\left(x^2 + y^2\right),</md>
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in miles from the center of the low. A ship sits at
      <m>P_0(20,10)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the pressure at the ship and <m>\nabla P</m> there. The
            pressure-gradient force per unit volume of air is a positive multiple of
            <m>-\nabla P</m>; in which unit direction does it push, and what is
            <m>\|\nabla P\|</m> in millibars per mile?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A second ship 40 miles from the center reports a stronger wind. Using this
            model, explain why <m>\|\nabla P\|</m> supports that report.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The captain decides to hold a constant barometer reading. Give the two
            possible unit headings at <m>P_0</m>, and describe the shape of the full route.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
  </statement>
  <!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>P(20,10) = 988 + 0.004(500) = 990</m> mb, and
            <m>\nabla P = 0.008x\,\hat{\imath} + 0.008y\,\hat{\jmath}</m> gives
            <m>(\nabla P)_{(20,10)} = 0.16\,\hat{\imath} + 0.08\,\hat{\jmath}</m>. The force
            points along
            <m>-\mathbf u = -\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}(2\,\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath})</m>, that is,
            inward toward the low, and
            <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.08\sqrt{5} \approx 0.18</m> mb/mi.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Here <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.008\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> grows linearly with distance from
            the center, so at 40 miles it is <m>0.32</m> mb/mi, nearly double the value at
            the first ship. (Real storms reverse this trend close to the eye; the model is
            only reasonable over a limited annulus.)
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf u = \pm\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}(\hat{\imath} - 2\,\hat{\jmath})</m>. Since the
            level curves of <m>P</m> are the circles <m>x^2+y^2 = \text{constant}</m>,
            holding the barometer steady means circling the storm at a fixed radius of
            <m>\sqrt{500} \approx 22.4</m> miles.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p component="web">
      Use the interactive figure to explore the problem further geometrically,
      switching between the two headings and watching how the barometer responds as
      the ship moves.
    </p>
    <p component="print">
      An interactive figure of the pressure field <mdash/> the two headings
      switchable, with the barometer responding as the ship moves <mdash/>
      accompanies the online version of these notes.
    </p>
    <sidebyside component="web" width="100%">
    <figure xml:id="figure-isobars-low">
      <caption>The pressure field <m>P(x,y) = 988 + 0.004(x^2+y^2)</m> around a low. One heading circles the eye at constant <m>990</m> mb; the other runs outward along <m>\nabla P</m>. The distance readout shows why <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.008r</m> grows with radius.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-isobars-low"
                   iframe="isobars-low-3d.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="16:10"/>
    </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </answer>
  END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the old worksheet "Exercises for Tangent Planes and
     Differentials" ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="rev-tp-1">
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation for the tangent plane to the graph of
<m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy)</m> passing through the point
<m>\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}, 1, 0\right)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
We have <m>f_x = -y\sin(xy)</m> and <m>f_y = -x\sin(xy)</m>, so the
tangent plane at a general point <m>(x_0, y_0)</m> is given by
<md>
z - \cos(x_0 y_0)
= -y_0 \sin(x_0 y_0)(x - x_0)
- x_0 \sin(x_0 y_0)(y - y_0).
</md>
</p>

<p>
Plugging in our point <m>\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}, 1, 0\right)</m>, this
becomes
<md>
z = -1\cdot\left(x - \frac{\pi}{2}\right)
- \frac{\pi}{2}\cdot(y - 1),
</md>
or equivalently
<md>
x + \frac{\pi}{2}\,y + z = \pi.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-tp-2">
<statement>
<p>
Find a parametric equation for a line contained in the tangent plane
you found in the previous problem. (Any line will suffice.)
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
Note that the point <m>\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}, 1, 0\right)</m> lies on
this plane, and the plane has normal vector
<m>\vec n = \left\langle 1, \dfrac{\pi}{2}, 1\right\rangle</m>. Any
vector orthogonal to the normal vector will point along this plane. By
inspection, we see that
<md>
\langle 1, 0, -1\rangle \cdot
\left\langle 1, \frac{\pi}{2}, 1\right\rangle = 0,
</md>
so the vector <m>\langle 1, 0, -1\rangle</m> points along the plane.
Thus <m>\langle 1, 0, -1\rangle</m> is the direction vector of a line
pointing along this plane. Combining this with the point on the plane
that we have already found, we see that the line
<md>
\mathbf r(t) = \left\langle t + \frac{\pi}{2},\; 1,\; -t\right\rangle
</md>
is contained in the tangent plane. We could find other lines contained
in the plane by making different choices of direction vector. See
<xref ref="fig-rev-tp-line"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-tp-line">
<caption>
The tangent plane <m>x + \frac{\pi}{2}y + z = \pi</m> to the graph of
<m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy)</m> at <m>P_0\left(\frac{\pi}{2},1,0\right)</m>,
its normal vector <m>\vec n = \langle 1, \frac{\pi}{2}, 1\rangle</m>,
and the line <m>\mathbf r(t) = \langle t + \frac{\pi}{2}, 1, -t\rangle</m>
contained in the plane.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional coordinate system showing a tilted shaded
parallelogram that represents the tangent plane. A red point P zero at
pi over two comma one comma zero lies in the middle of the plane. A
blue arrow perpendicular to the plane at P zero is the normal vector
n, and a dark red line through P zero lying inside the plane is the
line r of t.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (3.4,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0,2.9,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0,0,3.2) node[above] {$z$};
  % tangent plane patch: z = pi - x - (pi/2) y over x in [0.6,2.4], y in [0.3,1.7]
  \fill[msky, opacity=0.30]
    (0.6,0.3,2.0705) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.4,0.3,0.2705) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.4,1.7,-1.9285) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0.6,1.7,-0.1285) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ cycle;
  \draw[msky!70!black]
    (0.6,0.3,2.0705) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.4,0.3,0.2705) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.4,1.7,-1.9285) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0.6,1.7,-0.1285) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ cycle;
