</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-9">
<statement>
<p>
Find the equation of the tangent plane at the point <m>P_0(2,0,2)</m>
on the surface <m>2z - x^2 = 0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
Let <m>w(x,y,z) = 2z - x^2 = 0</m>. Then
<md>
\nabla w = (-2x)\,\mathbf i + 2\,\mathbf k,
</md>
and the normal to the tangent plane is
<md>
\vec n = \nabla w\big|_{P_0} = -4\,\mathbf i + 2\,\mathbf k.
</md>
The constant is
<md>
D = \vec n \cdot \vec{OP_0}
= \langle -4, 0, 2\rangle \cdot \langle 2, 0, 2\rangle
= -8 + 4 = -4,
</md>
so from <m>Ax + By + Cz = D</m>, the tangent plane at
<m>P_0(2,0,2)</m> is
<md>
-4x + 2z = -4
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
2x - z = 2.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-8-a">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement>
<p>
Any tangent plane to a graph must meet that graph at exactly one
point.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<answer>
<p>False.</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
Consider the plane <m>z = c</m>. The tangent
plane to this graph at any point is the same as <m>z = c</m>, so it
meets the graph at infinitely many points.
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-tp-3">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>S</m> be a sphere centered at the origin in <m>\R^3</m>, and
consider any point <m>P</m> on <m>S</m>. Show that the vector
<m>\vec{OP}</m> is orthogonal to the tangent plane to <m>S</m> at
<m>P</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
Let <m>R</m> be the radius of the sphere, and write
<m>P = (x_0, y_0, z_0)</m>. Assume for the moment that <m>P</m> lies on
the top half of the sphere (i.e. <m>z_0 \gt 0</m>). Note that the top
half of the sphere is the same as the graph of the function
<m>f(x,y) = \sqrt{R^2 - x^2 - y^2}</m>. We can compute
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0, y_0)
\amp = -\frac{x_0}{\sqrt{R^2 - x_0^2 - y_0^2}},</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(x_0, y_0)
\amp = -\frac{y_0}{\sqrt{R^2 - x_0^2 - y_0^2}},</mrow>
</md>
so that the tangent plane to the graph at <m>P</m> (which is the same
as the tangent plane to <m>S</m> at <m>P</m>) is given by
<md>
z - z_0
= -\frac{x_0}{\sqrt{R^2 - x_0^2 - y_0^2}}\,(x - x_0)
- \frac{y_0}{\sqrt{R^2 - x_0^2 - y_0^2}}\,(y - y_0).
</md>
</p>

<p>
We can rewrite this as
<md>
x_0(x - x_0) + y_0(y - y_0) + z_0(z - z_0) = 0,
</md>
where we use the fact that <m>z_0 = \sqrt{R^2 - x_0^2 - y_0^2}</m> to
simplify things. From this we see that
<m>\langle x_0, y_0, z_0\rangle</m> is a normal vector to the tangent
plane. But <m>\langle x_0, y_0, z_0\rangle = \vec{OP}</m>, so this is
exactly what we needed to show. See <xref ref="fig-rev-tp-sphere"/>.
</p>

<p>
A similar argument (with <m>-\sqrt{R^2 - x^2 - y^2}</m> in place of
<m>\sqrt{R^2 - x^2 - y^2}</m>) works when <m>P</m> is on the bottom
half of the sphere (i.e. <m>z_0 \lt 0</m>). When <m>z_0 = 0</m>, we
have to use a <q>sideways graph</q> of some function like
<m>f(y,z) = \sqrt{R^2 - y^2 - z^2}</m>, but other than that, pretty
much everything is the same.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rev-tp-sphere">
<caption>
The vector <m>\vec{OP} = \langle x_0, y_0, z_0\rangle</m> from the
center of the sphere to the point <m>P</m> is a normal vector to the
tangent plane to <m>S</m> at <m>P</m>.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<description>
<p>
A sphere centered at the origin O, drawn with a dashed equator. A
point P sits on the upper right of the sphere. An arrow from O through
P continues past the surface, and a small shaded parallelogram at P,
perpendicular to the arrow, represents the tangent plane. A right
angle marker at P shows that the vector O P is orthogonal to the
tangent plane.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % the sphere
  \draw[thick, mblue] (0,0) circle (2);
  \draw[mblue, dashed] (-2,0) arc (180:360:2 and 0.55);
  \draw[mblue] (-2,0) arc (180:0:2 and -0.55);
  % center
  \fill (0,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below left=1pt] {$O$};
  % the point P at 40 degrees
  \coordinate (P) at (40:2);
  % OP vector, extended past the sphere
  \draw[-stealth, mred, very thick] (0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (40:2.9)
        node[above right=-1pt] {$\vec{OP}$};
  \fill[red] (P) circle (2pt) node[below right=2pt] {$P$};
  % tangent plane at P: parallelogram perpendicular to OP
  % direction along the page perpendicular to OP: (-sin40, cos40)
  % slanted second direction to fake 3D
  \begin{scope}[shift={(P)}]
    \coordinate (u) at (-0.766,0.643);   % perpendicular to OP, unit
    \coordinate (v) at (0.30,0.42);      % skewed depth direction
    \fill[morange, opacity=0.30]
      ($-1.3*(u) - 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($1.3*(u) - 0.9*(v)$)
      ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($1.3*(u) + 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($-1.3*(u) + 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ cycle;
    \draw[morange!70!black]
      ($-1.3*(u) - 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($1.3*(u) - 0.9*(v)$)
      ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($1.3*(u) + 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($-1.3*(u) + 0.9*(v)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ cycle;
    % right-angle marker between OP direction and the plane direction u
    \draw ($0.34*(u)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ ($0.34*(u) + (0.26,0.218)$) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0.26,0.218);
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-33">
<statement>
<p>
Find the equation of the tangent plane to the sphere
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 14</m> at <m>(1,2,3)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
Write the sphere as the level surface
<m>F(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 14</m>. The gradient is normal to the
level surface:
<md>
\nabla F = \langle 2x, 2y, 2z\rangle,
\qquad
\vec n = \nabla F\big|_{(1,2,3)} = \langle 2, 4, 6\rangle.
</md>
Hence the tangent plane is
<md>
2(x-1) + 4(y-2) + 6(z-3) = 0,
</md>
which simplifies to <m>x + 2y + 3z = 14</m>. (Notice that the normal
vector is parallel to the position vector <m>\langle 1,2,3\rangle</m>,
exactly as we proved for spheres centered at the origin.)
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-34">
<statement>
<p>
The tangent plane to <m>z = f(x,y)</m> at the point <m>(1,2,9)</m> is
<m>z = 3x + 5y - 4</m>. Find <m>\nabla f(1,2)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
The tangent plane to the graph of <m>f</m> at <m>(a,b)</m> is
<md>
z = f(a,b) + f_x(a,b)(x-a) + f_y(a,b)(y-b),
</md>
so once it is expanded the coefficient of <m>x</m> is
<m>f_x(a,b)</m> and the coefficient of <m>y</m> is
<m>f_y(a,b)</m>. Comparing with <m>z = 3x + 5y - 4</m> gives
<md>
f_x(1,2) = 3, \qquad f_y(1,2) = 5,
</md>
and therefore
<md>
\nabla f(1,2) = 3\,\mathbf i + 5\,\mathbf j.
</md>
(As a check, the plane passes through <m>(1,2,9)</m>:
<m>3(1) + 5(2) - 4 = 9</m> <m>\checkmark</m>.)
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-36">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y) = 2 + \ln(1 + 3x - 4y)</m>. Use a linearization to
approximate <m>f(0.1, 0.2)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
The point <m>(0.1, 0.2)</m> is close to the origin, where the
function is easy to work with, so we linearize at <m>(0,0)</m>:
<md>
f(0,0) = 2 + \ln(1) = 2,
\qquad
f_x = \frac{3}{1+3x-4y},
\qquad
f_y = \frac{-4}{1+3x-4y},
</md>
so <m>f_x(0,0) = 3</m> and <m>f_y(0,0) = -4</m>. The linearization is
<md>
L(x,y) = f(0,0) + f_x(0,0)x + f_y(0,0)y = 2 + 3x - 4y,
</md>
and therefore
<md>
f(0.1, 0.2) \approx L(0.1, 0.2) = 2 + 0.3 - 0.8 = 1.5.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q1-2">
<statement>
<p>
Let
<md>
g(x,y) = \frac{\sqrt{x}}{\sqrt[3]{y}} ,
</md>
and note that <m>g(36,8) = 3</m>. Use an appropriate partial derivative
to approximate <m>g(36,6)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
We move from <m>(36,8)</m> to <m>(36,6)</m>. Only <m>y</m> changes, and
it changes by
<md>
\Delta y = 6 - 8 = -2 ,
</md>
so the partial derivative we need is <m>g_y</m>. Writing
<m>g(x,y) = x^{1/2} y^{-1/3}</m>,
<md>
g_y(x,y) = -\frac{1}{3}\, x^{1/2} y^{-4/3}
         = -\frac{\sqrt{x}}{3\, y^{4/3}} .
</md>
</p>

<p>
At <m>(36,8)</m> we have <m>\sqrt{36} = 6</m> and
<m>8^{4/3} = \left(8^{1/3}\right)^4 = 2^4 = 16</m>, so
<md>
g_y(36,8) = -\frac{6}{3 \cdot 16} = -\frac{6}{48} = -\frac{1}{8} .
</md>
</p>

<p>
The linear approximation is
<md>
<mrow>g(36,6) \amp \approx g(36,8) + g_y(36,8)\,\Delta y</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 3 + \left(-\frac{1}{8}\right)(-2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 3 + \frac14 = \frac{13}{4} = 3.25 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
As a check, the exact value is
<m>g(36,6) = 6/\sqrt[3]{6} \approx 3.302</m>, so the approximation is
good to about <m>1.6\%</m>.
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

</worksheet>